# The linter reports what the compiler already knows but has no reason to say: code that
# is written, compiled, and never reached. Every check here is answered from the parsed
# File alone — no types, no flow analysis — which keeps it honest about what it can claim.
#
# Every finding carries a CODE, so it can be named — in a diagnostic, in a review, in a
# suppression. The prefix groups them the way the formatter's does (see fmt.zg): `L1xx` is
# dead code, things written that nothing reaches.
#
#   L101  an unused import — the module is read, parsed, and merged into the program for
#         nothing, and it lies to the next reader about what this file depends on
#   L102  an unused private function — a public one is a module's interface and may have
#         no caller in this program, but a private one with no caller is simply dead
#   L103  a binding that is never read — the value was computed for nobody
#   L104  `_ := expr` — a discard binder over an expression that is ALREADY a statement, so
#         the binder is the one part of the line nothing reaches
#   L105  `with e as x { … }` where nothing in the block reads `x` — the block already scopes
#         the resource, so the name buys nothing and costs the next reader a question
#
# `L2xx` is NULL SAFETY: an optional handled in a way that works but says the wrong thing.
# Nothing here is a compile error — each of these programs runs — which is exactly why a
# linter is where they belong.
#
#   L201  `?? nil` — a fallback that is itself the absent value, so the `??` changes
#         nothing: the result is optional either way
#   L202  `x!` inside a function whose result is a `T?` — the absence can be HANDED BACK
#         with `?` instead of turned into an abort, and `!` is the easier key to reach
#
# `L4xx` is RESOLUTION: a name that answers to more than one thing.
#
#   L402  a `mut fn` that never writes through `this` — it makes every call site hold the
#         instance in a `mut` binding and gives nothing back
#
# L401 was a variant name two enums declare, and is RETIRED — see the range's own section
# below for what the language did to its reason.
#
# `L3xx` is CAPTURE: what a coroutine or a deferred call actually took.
#
#   L301  a `mut` binding captured by `spawn` / `defer` and WRITTEN after the capture —
#         the arguments are a snapshot taken where the statement is written, so the later
#         value is not the one the call sees. A channel is reported too, and only for a
#         REBINDING: a send is not a write, and after `ch = <another>` the coroutine holds
#         the old channel.
#
# `L5xx` is CONVERSION: a value that changed type, or a literal that took one, without the
# source saying so. docs/core/types.md asks for it over `Into` — "every implicit conversion is
# a lint finding, literals included" — and the reason is a reading one: `1` and `1.0` should
# mean different types on the page, so `1.5 + 1` is reported and `1.5 + 1.0` is not.
#
#   L502  a LITERAL took a type that is not its own — `b: byte = 5`, or the `1` in `1.5 + 1`
#
# `L501` STOOD BESIDE IT AND HAS RETIRED. It reported a VALUE that converted — `f: float = i`
# for an `int` binding — which was legal, one step, and invisible. It is no longer legal: a
# position wraps a value and never converts one (docs/core/type-system.md), so the finding's
# whole subject is a refusal now, and a lint whose programs the compiler rejects reports
# nothing on any program it is given. What it was for, `float(i)` said out loud, is what the
# language requires.
#
# THIS ONE IS NOT ANSWERED HERE, and it is the only one that is not. Everything above comes
# from the parsed File; a literal's adopted type is a fact about TYPES, so the LOWERING WALK
# records it and lint_conversions (emit.zg) hands it back. lint_program asks both and hands
# back one list.
#
# --- severity, and the two exit codes it decides -----------------------------------------
#
# A finding has a SEVERITY, and there are three:
#
#   a finding   the default. A rule fired on the program, which is what the tool is for, and
#               `zerg lint` exits nonzero — so it gates a build rather than decorating one.
#   warning     printed, and `zerg lint` still exits 0. It is what a rule says about a
#               program that is NOT wrong — something legal that the writer may have meant
#               and may not.
#   info        printed, and nothing exits nonzero for it, ever. A tidying job, not a defect.
#
# `make lint` — this project's gate over its OWN source — passes `--strict` and fails on a
# warning too. A gate board stricter than the tool has precedent here: refuse-check asserts
# more than `zerg build` requires.
#
# ONLY THE DEFAULT PRINTS NO ADJECTIVE. A finding is what `zerg lint` is for and needs no
# word in front of it; the two that do NOT fail the tool say which they are, because a line
# that does not change the exit status has to say so or it reads as one that did.

# Finding is one thing the linter has to say, with the place to say it about. It is not a
# `Diag`: a Diag is what a COMPILER stage produces and every one of those fails the build,
# which is the one property a lint finding does not have. The severity is the difference and
# it is the only field Diag has no room for.
pub struct Finding {
	# sev is the word printed before the code — "warning", "info", or "" for a finding. It is
	# the printed spelling rather than a tag mapped to one, so there is no second table
	# deciding what a severity is called.
	pub sev: str

	pub path: str
	pub line: int
	pub col: int
	pub code: str
	pub msg: str
}

# lint_program reports every finding across a loaded program (the entry file plus the modules
# it imports, as the driver resolved them), with the `#[allow(…)]` suppressions applied and
# the two things worth saying about a suppression itself.
pub fn lint_program(srcs: list[str], paths: list[str]) -> list[Finding] {
	# THE BUILT-IN SPECS TOO, and through the same function the lowering walk uses: `Into` is
	# declared by the language, so a program that implements it declares no spec by that name
	# — and L102 read the resulting `into` as a private function nobody calls.
	f := c_with_builtin_specs(merge_files_at(srcs, paths))
	mut out: list[Finding] = []

	# Names used ANYWHERE in the program: a private function called from another module
	# of the same program is used, so the whole merged File is the unit of judgement.
	mut ids: list[str] = []
	mut calls: list[str] = []
	mut ns: list[str] = []
	for fd in f.fns {
		walk_body(fd.body, ids, calls, ns)
	}
	lint_imports(f, ns, ids, out)
	lint_dead_fns(f, calls, ids, out)
	lint_unread_bindings(f, out)
	lint_captures(f, out)
	lint_optionals(f, out)
	lint_idle_mut_fn(f, out)
	lint_with_names(srcs, paths, out)

	# THE L5xx FAMILY IS ASKED HERE rather than by the driver. It used to be a second call the
	# driver made and a second loop it printed, which meant the suppression below would have
	# had to be applied twice, in two places, by whoever remembered — and `#[allow(L502)]` is
	# exactly the suppression a reader reaches for first.
	#
	# GUARDED, because lowering a program is the one thing here that can refuse. Its own rule
	# is already "a program that does not compile answers nothing" — it returns [] when the
	# walk collected diagnostics — but a form the emitter turns away by RAISING never reaches
	# that line, and the raise took the whole run with it. Everything above comes from the
	# parsed tree and is true of a program the emitter cannot lower, so the honest answer is
	# no conversions rather than no lint.
	mut convs: list[Diag] = []
	guard {
		convs = lint_conversions(srcs, paths)
	}
	for d in convs {
		out.append(Finding("", d.path, d.line, d.col, d.code, d.msg))
	}

	return out
}

# lint_with_names is L105: `with e as x { … }` where nothing in the block reads `x`. The
# block already scopes the resource — that is what `with` IS — so the name buys nothing and
# costs the next reader the question "where is `x` used?". The nameless form says the same
# thing and says it shorter.
#
# It reads TOKENS rather than the AST, and that is not a shortcut: `with` expands in the
# parser (GRAMMAR defines it by expansion), so by the time there is an AST there is no
# `with` left to lint. Every rule about this form is a token rule for the same reason —
# F409 next door is the other one.
fn lint_with_names(srcs: list[str], paths: list[str], mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	mut k := 0
	for k < srcs.len() {
		toks := tokenize(srcs[k])
		path := lint_path_at(paths, k)
		mut i := 0
		for i + 3 < toks.len() {
			if toks[i].kind == Kind.With {
				at := lint_with_as(toks, i)
				if at > 0 {
					name := toks[at].lexeme
					if not lint_reads_name(toks, at + 1, name) {
						out.append(Finding("", path, toks[at].line, toks[at].col, "L105", f"`with … as {name}` — nothing in the block reads the name, and the block already scopes the resource"))
					}
				}
			}
			i = i + 1
		}
		k = k + 1
	}
}

# lint_path_at is the file a source was read from, and "" when the caller had none to give.
# The two lists are parallel by construction (load_sources_at fills both), so the guard is
# about a caller that hands over sources alone rather than about a length that can drift.
fn lint_path_at(paths: list[str], i: int) -> str {
	return "" if i >= paths.len()

	return paths[i]
}

# lint_with_as answers the index of the identifier a `with` binds, or -1 when it binds none.
# The `as` is what it looks for, at the same bracket depth the `with` opened at, before the
# block's `{`.
fn lint_with_as(toks: list[Token], at: int) -> int {
	mut depth := 0
	mut i := at + 1
	for i + 1 < toks.len() {
		k := toks[i].kind
		if k == Kind.LParen or k == Kind.LBrack {
			depth = depth + 1
		} else if k == Kind.RParen or k == Kind.RBrack {
			depth = depth - 1
		} else if depth == 0 {
			return i + 1 if k == Kind.As and toks[i + 1].kind == Kind.Ident
			return 0 - 1 if k == Kind.LBrace or k == Kind.Semi
		}
		i = i + 1
	}
	return 0 - 1
}

# lint_reads_name reports whether the block opening at or after `at` mentions `name`. The
# block's own `{` is found first, so a mention in the HEAD — which cannot be a read of a
# binding the head is still making — does not count.
fn lint_reads_name(toks: list[Token], at: int, name: str) -> bool {
	mut i := at
	for i < toks.len() and toks[i].kind != Kind.LBrace {
		i = i + 1
	}
	return false if i >= toks.len()

	mut depth := 0
	i = i + 1
	for i < toks.len() {
		k := toks[i].kind
		if k == Kind.LBrace {
			depth = depth + 1
		} else if k == Kind.RBrace {
			return false if depth == 0

			depth = depth - 1
		} else if k == Kind.Ident and toks[i].lexeme == name {
			return true
		}
		i = i + 1
	}
	return false
}

# lint_idle_mut_fn reports a `mut fn` whose body never writes through `this`.
#
# `mut fn` is not a hint: it makes the receiver a `mut &`, so EVERY call site has to hold
# the instance in a `mut` binding (GRAMMAR#fn-decl). A method that only reads charges its
# callers that and gives nothing back — and the caller cannot see why, because the
# signature is the whole contract and `mut fn` is all it says.
#
# The test is a WRITE to `this`, not a mention of it: a `mut fn` that reads `this` is the
# ordinary case, and one that passes it to a `mut &` parameter is writing through it.
fn lint_idle_mut_fn(f: File, mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	refs := lint_ref_params(f)
	for fd in f.fns {
		if fd.recv != "" and lint_recv_is_mut(fd) and not writes_this(fd.body, refs) {
			out.append(Finding("", fd.path, fd.line, fd.col, "L402", f"`{fd.recv}.{fd.name}` is a `mut fn` and never writes through `this` — every call site has to hold the instance in a `mut` binding for nothing; make it a plain `fn`"))
		}
	}
}

fn lint_recv_is_mut(fd: FnDecl) -> bool {
	return false if fd.params.len() == 0
	return fd.params[0].is_ref
}

# writes_this is assigns_to over a whole body, including the blocks a statement carries.
fn writes_this(body: list[Stmt], refs: list[str]) -> bool {
	mut i := 0
	for i < body.len() {
		return true if assigns_to(body[i], "this", refs) or writes_this_in(body[i], refs)
		i = i + 1
	}
	return false
}

fn writes_this_in(s: Stmt, refs: list[str]) -> bool {
	return match s {
		Stmt.SIf(c, tb, he, eb) => writes_this(tb, refs) or writes_this(eb, refs)
		Stmt.SIfLet(nm, v, tb, he, eb) => writes_this(tb, refs) or writes_this(eb, refs)
		Stmt.SFor(hc, c, b) => writes_this(b, refs)
		Stmt.SForIn(nm, it, b) => writes_this(b, refs)
		Stmt.SSelect(arms) => writes_this_arms(arms, refs)
		Stmt.SForSelect(arms) => writes_this_arms(arms, refs)
		_ => false
	}
}

fn writes_this_arms(arms: list[SelArm], refs: list[str]) -> bool {
	for a in arms {
		return true if writes_this(a.body, refs)
	}
	return false
}

# --- L4xx: a name that resolves to more than one thing ------------------------------
#
# L401 IS RETIRED AND NO LONGER EMITTED. It reported a variant name two enums declare, on
# the reading that a bare use of it resolved to whichever enum was declared first — so
# `c := Red` was a coin toss and moving an `enum` changed what a program meant.
#
# Neither half of that survives. A variant is named THROUGH its enum (E383, c_bare_variant),
# so a bare `Red` is not a value in either enum; and a qualified `Signal.Red` is resolved
# inside the enum it names (c_var_row_in), so two enums that both declare a `Red` declare
# two different variants that are spelled alike and never compete. A lint whose reason has
# been taken away by the language is noise pointing at a program that is right, and its
# advice — write `Signal.Red` — is what the compiler now takes.

# --- L2xx: null safety -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Both checks are answered from the parsed File alone, like every other rule here. That is
# what keeps them honest: `?? nil` is a SHAPE, and so is a `!` inside a function whose
# declared result is a `T?`. Neither needs to know a type that was not written down.
fn lint_optionals(f: File, mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	for fd in f.fns {
		lint_opt_body(fd.body, LWhere(fd.path, fd.line, fd.col, fd.name), c_ret_is_opt(fd), out)
	}
}

# LWhere is the place a rule is standing at while it walks — the file, the statement the SAt
# marker last named, and the function the walk is inside. It is one value rather than four
# parameters because every walk here threads it through five or six helpers, and four
# parameters repeated six times is where a place gets dropped by whoever adds the seventh.
struct LWhere {
	pub path: str
	pub line: int
	pub col: int
	pub fname: str
}

# lint_step is `w` moved to the statement a marker names, and left alone for anything else.
# A block's statements each carry one (parse_block), so walking a body means asking this at
# every step and the place is exact without any rule tracking it.
fn lint_step(w: LWhere, s: Stmt) -> LWhere {
	return match s {
		Stmt.SAt(l, c) => LWhere(w.path, l, c, w.fname)
		_ => w
	}
}

fn c_ret_is_opt(fd: FnDecl) -> bool {
	return false if not fd.has_ret
	return match fd.ret {
		Ty.TOpt(_) => true
		_          => false
	}
}

fn lint_opt_body(body: list[Stmt], at: LWhere, ret_opt: bool, mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	mut w := at
	for s in body {
		w = lint_step(w, s)
		match s {
			Stmt.SLet(nm, ty, ht, im, ic, v) => lint_opt_expr(v, w, ret_opt, out)
			Stmt.SAssign(lhs, rhs) => lint_opt_expr(rhs, w, ret_opt, out)
			Stmt.SPrint(v) => lint_opt_expr(v, w, ret_opt, out)
			Stmt.SReturn(hv, v) => lint_opt_expr(v, w, ret_opt, out)
			Stmt.SExpr(v) => lint_opt_expr(v, w, ret_opt, out)
			Stmt.SIf(c, tb, he, eb) => lint_opt_two(tb, eb, w, ret_opt, out)
			Stmt.SIfLet(nm, v, tb, he, eb) => lint_opt_two(tb, eb, w, ret_opt, out)
			Stmt.SFor(hc, c, b) => lint_opt_body(b, w, ret_opt, out)
			Stmt.SForIn(nm, it, b) => lint_opt_body(b, w, ret_opt, out)
			Stmt.SSelect(arms) => lint_opt_arms(arms, w, ret_opt, out)
			Stmt.SForSelect(arms) => lint_opt_arms(arms, w, ret_opt, out)
			_ => lint_skip()
		}
	}
}

fn lint_opt_two(a: list[Stmt], b: list[Stmt], w: LWhere, ret_opt: bool, mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	lint_opt_body(a, w, ret_opt, out)
	lint_opt_body(b, w, ret_opt, out)
}

fn lint_opt_arms(arms: list[SelArm], w: LWhere, ret_opt: bool, mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	for a in arms {
		lint_opt_body(a.body, w, ret_opt, out)
	}
}

fn lint_opt_expr(e: Expr, w: LWhere, ret_opt: bool, mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	match e {
		Expr.ECoalesce(a, b, jump) => lint_opt_coalesce(a, b, w, ret_opt, out)
		Expr.EForce(x) => lint_opt_force(x, w, ret_opt, out)
		Expr.EBinary(op, a, b) => lint_opt_pair(a, b, w, ret_opt, out)
		Expr.EUnary(op, x) => lint_opt_expr(x, w, ret_opt, out)
		Expr.EField(t, nm) => lint_opt_expr(t, w, ret_opt, out)
		Expr.EOptChain(t, nm) => lint_opt_expr(t, w, ret_opt, out)
		Expr.ETry(x) => lint_opt_expr(x, w, ret_opt, out)
		Expr.ECall(nm, args, _) => lint_opt_args(args, w, ret_opt, out)
		Expr.EMethod(t, nm, args) => lint_opt_args(args, w, ret_opt, out)
		_ => lint_skip()
	}
}

fn lint_opt_pair(a: Expr, b: Expr, w: LWhere, ret_opt: bool, mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	lint_opt_expr(a, w, ret_opt, out)
	lint_opt_expr(b, w, ret_opt, out)
}

fn lint_opt_args(args: list[Expr], w: LWhere, ret_opt: bool, mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	for a in args {
		lint_opt_expr(a, w, ret_opt, out)
	}
}

fn lint_opt_coalesce(a: Expr, b: Expr, w: LWhere, ret_opt: bool, mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	if is_nil_expr(b) {
		out.append(Finding("", w.path, w.line, w.col, "L201", f"`?? nil` in `{w.fname}` changes nothing — the result is optional either way"))
	}
	lint_opt_expr(a, w, ret_opt, out)
	lint_opt_expr(b, w, ret_opt, out)
}

fn lint_opt_force(x: Expr, w: LWhere, ret_opt: bool, mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	if ret_opt {
		out.append(Finding("", w.path, w.line, w.col, "L202", f"`!` in `{w.fname}`, which answers a `T?` — `?` hands the absence back instead of aborting"))
	}
	lint_opt_expr(x, w, ret_opt, out)
}

fn is_nil_expr(e: Expr) -> bool {
	return match e {
		Expr.ENil => true
		_         => false
	}
}

fn lint_skip() {
	return
}

# --- L3xx: what a coroutine and a deferred call actually captured ------------------
#
# `spawn f(k)` and `defer f(k)` take their arguments as a SNAPSHOT, at the line they are
# written on. A write to `k` afterwards is not seen by the call — the coroutine may not
# even have started, and the deferred call has not run. That is the right semantics and it
# is the single most misreadable thing in this language: an experienced reader has to ask.
#
# So the tool answers instead of waiting to be asked. It is a lint and not an error because
# the program is correct and the snapshot is often exactly what was wanted.
#
# It looks WITHIN ONE BLOCK, at the statements after the capture. A write from a nested
# block, or from a later call, is not reported: this reports the shape that misleads,
# rather than every shape that could.
fn lint_captures(f: File, mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	refs := lint_ref_params(f)
	for fd in f.fns {
		mut muts: list[str] = []
		lint_captures_body(fd.body, LWhere(fd.path, fd.line, fd.col, fd.name), refs, muts, out)
	}
}

# lint_ref_params is `name#idx` for every `mut &` parameter in the program. A call is a
# WRITE to the variable it passes only in those slots, and the declaration is the only
# place that says which — without it, `show(k)` (a read) was reported as a write.
fn lint_ref_params(f: File) -> list[str] {
	mut refs: list[str] = []
	for fd in f.fns {
		mut j := 0
		for j < fd.params.len() {
			if fd.params[j].is_ref {
				refs.append(f"{fd.name}#{strconv.to_string(j, 10)}")
			}
			j = j + 1
		}
	}
	return refs
}

# `muts` is INHERITED by a nested block rather than reset in it. Resetting made the rule
# block-scoped by DECLARATION when the documented rule is block-scoped by STATEMENT: a
# `mut k` declared in a function and captured-then-written inside an `if` was silent, even
# though the capture and the write sat next to each other.
fn lint_captures_body(body: list[Stmt], at: LWhere, refs: list[str], mut &muts: list[str], mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	mut w := at
	mut i := 0
	for i < body.len() {
		w = lint_step(w, body[i])
		match body[i] {
			Stmt.SLet(nm, ty, has_ty, is_mut, is_const, v) => lint_captures_let(nm, is_mut, v, w, refs, muts, out)
			Stmt.SExpr(e) => lint_captures_expr(e, w, refs, muts, out)
			Stmt.SSpawn(x) => lint_capture_at(body, i, x, "spawn", w, refs, muts, out)
			Stmt.SDefer(x) => lint_capture_at(body, i, x, "defer", w, refs, muts, out)
			Stmt.SIf(c, tb, he, eb) => lint_captures_two(tb, eb, w, refs, muts, out)
			Stmt.SIfLet(nm, v, tb, he, eb) => lint_captures_two(tb, eb, w, refs, muts, out)
			Stmt.SFor(hc, c, b) => lint_captures_body(b, w, refs, muts, out)
			Stmt.SForIn(nm, it, b) => lint_captures_body(b, w, refs, muts, out)

			# a select arm's body is a block like any other, and it is where coroutines
			# actually live — the other two walks in this file grew an `_arms` helper for
			# the same reason, and this one was written without it
			Stmt.SSelect(arms) => lint_captures_arms(arms, w, refs, muts, out)
			Stmt.SForSelect(arms) => lint_captures_arms(arms, w, refs, muts, out)
			_ => lint_skip()
		}
		i = i + 1
	}
}

fn lint_captures_two(a: list[Stmt], b: list[Stmt], w: LWhere, refs: list[str], mut &muts: list[str], mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	lint_captures_body(a, w, refs, muts, out)
	lint_captures_body(b, w, refs, muts, out)
}

fn lint_captures_arms(arms: list[SelArm], w: LWhere, refs: list[str], mut &muts: list[str], mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	for a in arms {
		lint_captures_body(a.body, w, refs, muts, out)
	}
}

fn lint_captures_let(nm: str, is_mut: bool, v: Expr, w: LWhere, refs: list[str], mut &muts: list[str], mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	lint_note_mut(nm, is_mut, muts)
	lint_captures_expr(v, w, refs, muts, out)
}

# A closure's body and a `guard`'s are blocks no STATEMENT holds — they hang off an
# expression, so a walk that dispatches on statements alone never reaches them and this rule
# was silent inside every closure. `collect_binds_expr` grew the same descent for the same
# reason. A closure is reached through a call's arguments too, since `run(fn() { … })` is
# the shape they are actually written in.
fn lint_captures_expr(e: Expr, w: LWhere, refs: list[str], mut &muts: list[str], mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	match e {
		Expr.EGuard(b) => lint_captures_body(b, w, refs, muts, out)
		Expr.EBlock(b) => lint_captures_body(b, w, refs, muts, out)
		Expr.EFn(ps, has_ret, ret, b) => lint_captures_body(b, w, refs, muts, out)
		Expr.ECall(nm, args, _) => lint_captures_each(args, w, refs, muts, out)
		Expr.EMethod(t, m, args) => lint_captures_each(args, w, refs, muts, out)
		_ => lint_skip()
	}
}

fn lint_captures_each(args: list[Expr], w: LWhere, refs: list[str], mut &muts: list[str], mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	for a in args {
		lint_captures_expr(a, w, refs, muts, out)
	}
}

# lint_note_mut records a `mut` binding.
#
# A channel binding used to be EXEMPT, on the reasoning that a handle is shared and a later
# send is seen. The reasoning is right and the exemption was still wrong: a send is `ch <-
# v`, which is not an assignment and was never reported. The only thing that reaches this
# rule is `ch = <another channel>` — a REBINDING, after which the coroutine holds the old
# channel and everything sent on the new one goes nowhere near it. So the exemption
# suppressed nothing but correct reports.
fn lint_note_mut(nm: str, is_mut: bool, mut &muts: list[str]) {
	return if not is_mut
	muts.append(nm)
}

# lint_capture_at reports every `mut` name this statement captured that a LATER statement
# in the same block writes to.
fn lint_capture_at(body: list[Stmt], at: int, x: Expr, kw: str, w: LWhere, refs: list[str], muts: list[str], mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	# `spawn f(k, k)` and `f(k + k)` reach walk_expr as two occurrences of one name, and the
	# finding is about the NAME — reporting it twice would say the same thing twice
	mut said: list[str] = []
	for n in expr_idents(x) {
		if has_str(muts, n) and not has_str(said, n) and assigned_after(body, at, n, refs) {
			said.append(n)
			out.append(Finding("", w.path, w.line, w.col, "L301", f"`{n}` is captured by the `{kw}` in `{w.fname}` as it is HERE, and written after it — the call sees the value at the capture, not the later one"))
		}
	}
}

# expr_idents is the names an expression reads. walk_expr collects three kinds at once
# because its other callers want all three; this one wants the first.
fn expr_idents(x: Expr) -> list[str] {
	mut names: list[str] = []
	mut calls: list[str] = []
	mut ns: list[str] = []
	walk_expr(x, names, calls, ns)
	return names
}

# assigned_after is whether any statement after `at` assigns to `n`, in this block.
fn assigned_after(body: list[Stmt], at: int, n: str, refs: list[str]) -> bool {
	mut i := at + 1
	for i < body.len() {
		return true if assigns_to(body[i], n, refs)
		i = i + 1
	}
	return false
}

# assigns_to is whether a statement WRITES `n` — which is not only an assignment. A
# captured `list` is snapshotted by deep copy, so `xs.append(2)` after the capture is
# exactly the misreading this rule exists for, and it is a method call rather than an
# `SAssign`. `chk_root_name` is the checker's walk of an assignment target; this file had a
# verbatim copy of it, which meant the set of shapes that count as a target was written
# down twice.
fn assigns_to(s: Stmt, n: str, refs: list[str]) -> bool {
	return match s {
		Stmt.SAssign(lhs, rhs) => chk_root_name(lhs) == n
		Stmt.SExpr(e) => mutates_expr(e, n, refs)
		_ => false
	}
}

# mutates_expr is whether a bare expression statement writes `n`: a method that writes
# through its receiver, or a call passing it to a `mut &` parameter. Neither is an
# assignment and both are writes — but neither is EVERY call, which is what the first
# version of this said. `show(k)` is a read, and it was reported.
#
# `c_meth_mutates` is the emitter's one statement of which methods write through their
# receiver, and `refs` is read off the declarations. Both facts are asked of their owner
# rather than guessed at here, so a method or a signature added later is seen for free.
fn mutates_expr(e: Expr, n: str, refs: list[str]) -> bool {
	return match e {
		Expr.EMethod(t, m, args) => c_meth_mutates(m) and chk_root_name(t) == n
		Expr.ECall(nm, args, _) => ref_arg(nm, args, n, refs)
		_ => false
	}
}

# ref_arg is whether `nm` takes `n` in a `mut &` slot.
fn ref_arg(nm: str, args: list[Expr], n: str, refs: list[str]) -> bool {
	mut i := 0
	for i < args.len() {
		return true if chk_root_name(args[i]) == n and has_str(refs, f"{nm}#{strconv.to_string(i, 10)}")
		i = i + 1
	}
	return false
}

# lint_imports reports an import whose namespace nothing reaches through.
fn lint_imports(f: File, ns: list[str], ids: list[str], mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	for im in f.imports {
		# the BINDING, not the path's last segment: an `as` rename is the name this file
		# reaches the module by, so asking about anything else calls every renamed import
		# unused and lets one that genuinely is go unreported
		if not has_str(ns, im.name) and not has_str(ids, im.name) {
			out.append(Finding("", im.at, im.line, im.col, "L101", f"unused import \"{im.path}\""))
		}
	}
}

# lint_dead_fns reports a private function nothing calls. `main` is the program's entry —
# the runtime calls it, so it is never "unused" no matter what the source says.
fn lint_dead_fns(f: File, calls: list[str], ids: list[str], mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	reqs := spec_required_names(f)
	for fd in f.fns {
		# `init` is called by the generated main, the same way the runtime calls `main`
		if fd.is_pub or fd.name == "main" or is_init_name(fd.name) {
			continue_marker()
		} else if has_str(reqs, fd.name) {
			# A METHOD THAT SATISFIES A SPEC IS AN INTERFACE, the way a `pub` function is: what
			# calls it may be another program, or — for `Into` — the compiler, at a typed
			# position where nothing in the source spells the call at all. `impl Into[int] for
			# Celsius` reported its own `into` as dead code in a program that converted four
			# times.
			continue_marker()
		} else if not has_str(calls, fd.name) and not has_str(ids, fd.name) {
			out.append(Finding("", fd.path, fd.line, fd.col, "L102", f"private function `{fd.name}` is never called"))
		}
	}
}

# spec_required_names is every method name some spec in the program declares. It is asked of
# the SPECS rather than of the impls, and deliberately: a name is checked here, not a (type,
# name) pair, and matching more loosely errs toward saying nothing — which is the right
# direction for a rule whose finding is "delete this".
fn spec_required_names(f: File) -> list[str] {
	mut out: list[str] = []
	for sp in f.specs {
		for r in sp.reqs {
			out.append(r.name)
		}
	}
	return out
}

# lint_unread_bindings reports a binding whose name is never mentioned again inside the
# function that declared it. It is deliberately per-function and name-based: the subset
# has no shadowing rules to model, and a name that reappears anywhere in the body is
# enough to call the binding read.
#
# `_` is answered here too, as L104, and not by a walk of its own: this loop is already handed
# every name a function binds, `_` among them. An unread `_` is what `_` MEANS, so "never read"
# is the one thing there is no point saying about it — what is worth saying is that the BINDER
# has no reader, and that is the same fact this walk already has in hand.
fn lint_unread_bindings(f: File, mut &out: list[Finding]) {
	for fd in f.fns {
		mut names: list[str] = []
		mut ats: list[LWhere] = []
		collect_binds_at(fd.body, LWhere(fd.path, fd.line, fd.col, fd.name), names, ats)

		# THE BODY IS WALKED ONCE PER FUNCTION, not once per binding. The walk does not depend
		# on `n`, and it sat inside the loop — so a function with forty bindings walked its own
		# body forty times, and the rule cost the square of what it needed to.
		mut ids: list[str] = []
		mut calls: list[str] = []
		mut ns: list[str] = []
		walk_body(fd.body, ids, calls, ns)

		mut i := 0
		for i < names.len() {
			n := names[i]
			w := ats[i]
			if n == "_" {
				out.append(Finding("", w.path, w.line, w.col, "L104", f"`_ :=` in `{fd.name}` — the expression is already a statement, so the binder says nothing"))
			} else if not has_str(ids, n) {
				out.append(Finding("", w.path, w.line, w.col, "L103", f"binding `{n}` in `{fd.name}` is never read"))
			}
			i = i + 1
		}
	}
}

# --- collection ------------------------------------------------------------------------

# collect_binds gathers the names a block binds, descending into nested blocks. It is the
# name-only face of collect_binds_at, for the emitter's capture analysis, which wants the
# set and has no diagnostic to place.
fn collect_binds(body: list[Stmt], mut &names: list[str]) {
	mut ats: list[LWhere] = []
	collect_binds_at(body, LWhere("", 0, 0, ""), names, ats)
}

# collect_binds_at is the same walk, keeping WHERE each name was bound alongside it. The two
# lists are parallel: `ats[i]` is where `names[i]` was written, taken from the SAt marker the
# parser leaves before every statement of a block.
fn collect_binds_at(body: list[Stmt], at: LWhere, mut &names: list[str], mut &ats: list[LWhere]) {
	mut w := at
	for s in body {
		w = lint_step(w, s)
		match s {
			Stmt.SLet(name, ty, has_ty, is_mut, is_const, value) => collect_binds_let(name, value, w, names, ats)
			Stmt.SIf(cond, then_b, has_else, else_b) => collect_binds_if(then_b, else_b, w, names, ats)
			Stmt.SFor(is_cond, cond, body_b) => collect_binds_at(body_b, w, names, ats)
			Stmt.SForIn(name, iter, body_b) => collect_binds_at(body_b, w, names, ats)
			Stmt.SIfLet(name, value, then_b, has_else, else_b) => collect_binds_iflet(name, then_b, else_b, w, names, ats)
			Stmt.SExpr(x) => collect_binds_expr(x, w, names, ats)
			Stmt.SSelect(arms) => collect_binds_arms(arms, w, names, ats)
			Stmt.SForSelect(arms) => collect_binds_arms(arms, w, names, ats)
			_ => skip()
		}
	}
}

# collect_binds_arms reaches the bindings inside a select's arm bodies. Without it a binding
# made in an arm was invisible to L103 and L104 both — an arm body is a block like any other.
fn collect_binds_arms(arms: list[SelArm], w: LWhere, mut &names: list[str], mut &ats: list[LWhere]) {
	for a in arms {
		collect_binds_at(a.body, w, names, ats)
	}
}

fn collect_binds_let(name: str, value: Expr, w: LWhere, mut &names: list[str], mut &ats: list[LWhere]) {
	names.append(name)
	ats.append(w)
	collect_binds_expr(value, w, names, ats)
}

# collect_binds_expr descends into the one EXPRESSION that carries a block of its own.
# `guard` is an expression (GRAMMAR#guard-expr), so a binding made inside one is reached from the
# statement that holds the guard rather than from a statement of its own.
fn collect_binds_expr(e: Expr, w: LWhere, mut &names: list[str], mut &ats: list[LWhere]) {
	match e {
		Expr.EGuard(body_b) => collect_binds_at(body_b, w, names, ats)
		Expr.EBlock(body_b) => collect_binds_at(body_b, w, names, ats)

		# a closure's body is a block too, and `walk_body` already counts the reads inside one
		# — so without this the bindings in a closure were collected by nobody and answered to
		# neither rule
		Expr.EFn(ps, has_ret, ret, body_b) => collect_binds_at(body_b, w, names, ats)
		_ => skip()
	}
}

fn collect_binds_if(then_b: list[Stmt], else_b: list[Stmt], w: LWhere, mut &names: list[str], mut &ats: list[LWhere]) {
	collect_binds_at(then_b, w, names, ats)
	collect_binds_at(else_b, w, names, ats)
}

# collect_binds_iflet records the name `if v := e` binds as well as the blocks', since
# the binding is the whole reason the form exists and an unread one is still dead.
fn collect_binds_iflet(name: str, then_b: list[Stmt], else_b: list[Stmt], w: LWhere, mut &names: list[str], mut &ats: list[LWhere]) {
	names.append(name)
	ats.append(w)
	collect_binds_at(then_b, w, names, ats)
	collect_binds_at(else_b, w, names, ats)
}

# walk_body records every identifier read, every called function name, and every namespace
# a method call goes through, for a whole block.
fn walk_body(body: list[Stmt], mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	for s in body {
		match s {
			Stmt.SLet(name, ty, has_ty, is_mut, is_const, value) => walk_expr(value, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SAssign(target, value) => walk_pair(target, value, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SPrint(x) => walk_expr(x, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SReturn(has_value, value) => walk_expr(value, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SReturnIf(value, cond) => walk_pair(value, cond, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SExpr(x) => walk_expr(x, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SIf(cond, then_b, has_else, else_b) => walk_if(cond, then_b, else_b, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SIfLet(name, value, then_b, has_else, else_b) => walk_if(value, then_b, else_b, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SFor(is_cond, cond, body_b) => walk_loop(cond, body_b, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SForIn(name, iter, body_b) => walk_loop(iter, body_b, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SRaise(x, hc, c) => walk_raise(x, hc, c, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SDefer(x) => walk_expr(x, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SSpawn(x) => walk_expr(x, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SSend(ch, v) => walk_pair(ch, v, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SSelect(arms) => walk_sel(arms, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SForSelect(arms) => walk_sel(arms, ids, calls, ns)
			Stmt.SDel(n) => ids.append(n)
			Stmt.SClose(ch, at_exit) => walk_expr(ch, ids, calls, ns)
			_ => skip()
		}
	}
}

fn walk_pair(a: Expr, b: Expr, mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	walk_expr(a, ids, calls, ns)
	walk_expr(b, ids, calls, ns)
}

fn walk_if(cond: Expr, then_b: list[Stmt], else_b: list[Stmt], mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	walk_expr(cond, ids, calls, ns)
	walk_body(then_b, ids, calls, ns)
	walk_body(else_b, ids, calls, ns)
}

fn walk_loop(head: Expr, body_b: list[Stmt], mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	walk_expr(head, ids, calls, ns)
	walk_body(body_b, ids, calls, ns)
}

# walk_expr records the names an expression reaches for.
fn walk_sel(arms: list[SelArm], mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	for a in arms {
		walk_expr(a.ch, ids, calls, ns)
		walk_expr(a.val, ids, calls, ns)
		walk_body(a.body, ids, calls, ns)
	}
}

fn walk_two_lists(a: list[Expr], b: list[Expr], mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	walk_all(a, ids, calls, ns)
	walk_all(b, ids, calls, ns)
}

# walk_raise visits a raise's Err and, when it has one, its `from` cause — both are
# ordinary expressions, and a name used only as a cause is still a name used.
fn walk_raise(x: Expr, has_cause: bool, cause: Expr, mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	walk_expr(x, ids, calls, ns)
	if has_cause {
		walk_expr(cause, ids, calls, ns)
	}
}

fn walk_expr(e: Expr, mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	match e {
		Expr.EIdent(name) => ids.append(name)
		Expr.EUnary(oper, x) => walk_expr(x, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EBinary(oper, a, b) => walk_pair(a, b, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.ECall(name, args, _) => walk_call(name, args, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EField(target, name) => walk_expr(target, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EOptChain(target, name) => walk_expr(target, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.ETry(x) => walk_expr(x, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EIndex(target, idx) => walk_pair(target, idx, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EMethod(target, meth, args) => walk_method(target, meth, args, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EListLit(elems) => walk_all(elems, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EFill(v, n) => walk_pair(v, n, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EConv(ty, x, nm) => walk_expr(x, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EIf(cond, then_e, else_e) => walk_three(cond, then_e, else_e, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EMatch(scrut, arms) => walk_match(scrut, arms, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.ERange(lo, hi, inclusive) => walk_pair(lo, hi, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.ETuple(elems) => walk_all(elems, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EMapLit(ks, vs) => walk_two_lists(ks, vs, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EFn(ps, has_ret, ret, body) => walk_body(body, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EChanNew(t, cap) => walk_expr(cap, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.ERecv(ch) => walk_expr(ch, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EForce(x) => walk_expr(x, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.ECoalesce(a, b, jump) => walk_coalesce(a, b, jump, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EGuard(body) => walk_body(body, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EBlock(body) => walk_body(body, ids, calls, ns)

		# only the SUBJECT is a name the program bound — the right side of `is` is an error
		# kind, so counting it as a use would keep an unused import alive.
		Expr.EIs(x, name) => walk_expr(x, ids, calls, ns)
		Expr.EIn(x, set) => walk_pair(x, set, ids, calls, ns)
		_ => skip()
	}
}

# walk_coalesce reaches both halves of `a ?? b`: the fallback is either an expression or
# a divergent statement, and only one of the two is ever populated.
fn walk_coalesce(a: Expr, b: Expr, jump: list[Stmt], mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	walk_expr(a, ids, calls, ns)
	walk_expr(b, ids, calls, ns)
	walk_body(jump, ids, calls, ns)
}

fn walk_call(name: str, args: list[Expr], mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	calls.append(name)
	walk_all(args, ids, calls, ns)
}

# walk_method records the receiver of `x.m(...)`. When the receiver is a bare name it may
# be a MODULE namespace (`io.write(…)`) rather than a value, which is exactly what tells
# an import apart from a dead one — so it is recorded as both.
# walk_method records what a `target.meth(args)` reaches. The method NAME is a call: it
# may be a method on a value, and a private one of those has no other spelling that would
# mark it used — without this every private method reads as dead code. When the target is
# a bare identifier it is also recorded as a namespace, because the same syntax is how a
# module's function is reached, and that is what tells an import from an unused one.
fn walk_method(target: Expr, meth: str, args: list[Expr], mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	match target {
		Expr.EIdent(name) => ns.append(name)
		_ => skip()
	}
	calls.append(meth)
	walk_expr(target, ids, calls, ns)
	walk_all(args, ids, calls, ns)
}

fn walk_three(a: Expr, b: Expr, c: Expr, mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	walk_expr(a, ids, calls, ns)
	walk_expr(b, ids, calls, ns)
	walk_expr(c, ids, calls, ns)
}

fn walk_all(es: list[Expr], mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	for x in es {
		walk_expr(x, ids, calls, ns)
	}
}

fn walk_match(scrut: Expr, arms: list[Arm], mut &ids: list[str], mut &calls: list[str], mut &ns: list[str]) {
	walk_expr(scrut, ids, calls, ns)
	for a in arms {
		walk_expr(a.body, ids, calls, ns)
		if a.has_guard {
			walk_expr(a.guard_expr, ids, calls, ns)
		}
	}
}

# --- helpers -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn has_str(xs: list[str], want: str) -> bool {
	for x in xs {
		return true if x == want
	}
	return false
}

# skip is the no-op a match arm needs: an arm is an EXPRESSION, so a case with nothing to
# do still has to call something.
fn skip() {
	nop
}

# continue_marker stands in for a `continue` the subset's match-arm form cannot host.
fn continue_marker() {
	nop
}
